  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOne of my interests outside of meteorology is also something \ninvolved with looking at the sky. As I was driving into work this \nevening listening to classic rock (because I'm old), I heard a song \nthat fits with tonight, Harvest Moon by Neil Young. It looked \nbeautiful when I was driving in. Much of the area, not all but a\ndecent amount, will be mostly clear through sunrise so I highly\nsuggest going out and taking a look if you can.\n\nOtherwise, things look relatively tranquil today. A weak cold front \nwill approach from the north and this will bring some breezy \nconditions to some locations. And, there could be just enough \nmid level moisture for an isolated shower mainly in and near the\nmountains. The chance is now more than 1 in 10 and chances were\nadded mainly for fire weather purposes. Most locations will see\nnothing and remain dry through at least Sunday morning. It will\nalso be a warm day today, with high temperatures rising into \nthe 80s across the warmer spots like Worland and Thermopolis and\npossibly Casper. As for potential fire weather issues, relative\nhumidity should remain above critical levels. However, with \nvalues dropping to around 20 percent, locally elevated fire \nweather is possible in the breezier locations for a time this \nafternoon.\n\nThe next chance of rain moves in Sunday afternoon into Monday as \nsome moisture moves into the area, with an upper level low driving \nsome moisture from Hurricane Polo off of the Mexican coast.\nThis, along with some upper level divergence and the front \nbeing in the area. This does not look like a big rainfall event \nthough. This mornings model runs have come in somewhat drier, \nkeeping the bulk of the moisture to the south of Wyoming. Much \nof the southern and western portions of the area still have a \ngreater than 1 in 2 chance of over a tenth of an inch of rain, \nalthough this would fall over a greater than 1 day period. \nChances of greater than a quarter of an inch of rain greater \nthan 2 out of 5 are largely limited to the mountains and \npossibly the far southern portions of the area. And, we also \nhave to talk about a bit of snow. There is not a ton of cold air\nwith this system, with 700 millibar temperatures only falling \nto around 4 Celsius, which would put snow levels around 10000 \nfeet. There will likely be some snow in the air. Accumulations \nwould be light though. The NBM ensemble shows the only area with\na greater than 1 in 2 chance of an inch or more to be in the \nWind River Range, largely above 11500 feet with no impacts to \ntravel corridors. Most of the showers should end by sunrise on \nTuesday.\n\nFollowing that, largely tranquil conditions should move back into \nthe area for most of the period as ridging builds over the Northern\nRockies. That means another fairly lengthy period of above \nnormal temperatures and little chance of precipitation. We will \nwill have to keep an eye to the south though. It looks quite \nwet down there as an upper level low moving from California \ncombines with the remnants of Hurricane Polo to bring some \nsoaking rain and possible problems with flooding. Almost all \nguidance keeps this moisture to the south and east of western \nand central Wyoming. However, upper level lows and tropical \nsystems are fickle so we will continue to watch this. Looking \nway out to next weekend, we may really have a taste of fall as \none of the first Canadian cold fronts may drop southward into \nWyoming, bringing some much cooler temperatures. With the \ncontinental origin of the system, moisture looks limited though.\nIt is still a week out so a lot can change, but we may finally \nhave a decent taste of mid autumn Saturday or Sunday.",
